reference_csfset/ -- frozen CSFSET pair-eval oracle
====================================================

Scope and status
----------------
Add-only oracle frozen for the v1.0.0 release cycle (see
docs/SPEC_CSFSET_ORACLE.md, which is removed before the public v1.0.0 cut).
These files record the EXACT output the CURRENT (commit cb8a3f3) sub-DRT
CSFSET path produces for a representative set of CSFSET inputs. They change
no library code: the generator and consumer test use the public API only
(hexpr_drt_from_csfset / hexpr_subspace_eval / hexpr_pair_eval).

Why this exists
---------------
A future, internal change will replace CSFSET per-pair evaluation (today:
build a sub-DRT per pair) with a DRT-free direct per-pair L_k enumerator
(deferred to after v1.0.0). That rewrite needs a trusted baseline to diff
against. No committed artifact captured CSFSET arbitrary-input pair outputs
before this, so these files freeze the current behavior while the trusted
path exists. c/tests/test_csfset_pair.c re-runs every case and asserts a
match; the SAME unchanged test later guards that the rewrite reproduces the
frozen baseline byte/numeric-for-byte.

Trust basis -- what this oracle does and does NOT anchor
--------------------------------------------------------
This oracle is REGRESSION protection, not a physics proof.

  * Physical correctness is anchored ELSEWHERE and already stands: the
    per-pair coefficient is a path-only pure function (hmlkva);
    reference_pair/ (RubyRef-generated, SOCI/FOCI) anchors that coefficient
    kernel via test_pair_*; and end-to-end CI energies are validated against
    OpenMolcas to 1e-8 Ha (validation/). CSFSET pairs reuse the same kernel,
    so they inherit that anchoring.
  * This oracle anchors the CSFSET selection/indexing CONTRACT: it freezes
    the exact (bra_pos, ket_pos, ij, pqrs, coef) the current C path emits, in
    the input-order directional contract established by cb8a3f3, so the future
    rewrite can be proven to preserve that behavior.

RubyRef was considered as an alternative oracle source. It computes CSFSET
via the same sub-DRT algorithm but emits CANONICAL, symmetric indices with no
input-order contract, so using it as the contract baseline would require a
reconciliation layer -- itself a new error source. Physical-coefficient
confidence already comes from reference_pair + OpenMolcas, so the C-path
oracle is used for the contract and no RubyRef reconciliation layer is built.

The cases
---------
  O1   all 20 CSFs of s_full_4o4e, canonical order (subspace; full+one).
       Cross-checked against reference_pair/s_full_4o4e (Hermitian first-wins;
       638 unique keys, max|dcoef| = 0).
  O2   [uefd, eudf] (norb 4): non-canonical input order + canonical superset
       (M>N); pair + subspace; full+one.
  O5   [eeff, eeff] (norb 4): duplicate input row (N>M canonical dedup);
       pair + subspace; full.
  OF   9-CSF subset of s_foci_mid (core2/act2/ext2, nve6, FOCI), input order;
       subspace; full+one. Source rows (0-based) 0,5,13,16,21,29,32,37,40:
         0  ufeudd   5  ufuedd  13 uffeed  (core hole at orb0)
         16 fueudd  21 fuuedd  29 fufeed   (core hole at orb1)
         32 ffeeud  37 ffueed  40 fffeee   (cores full)
       spanning both core-hole sectors plus external particles, with the
       no-external reference CSF (40).
  OFs  the SAME 9 OF rows, deliberately shuffled (40,16,5,32,0,29,13,37,21);
       subspace; full. Input-order contract: OFs yields the identical canonical
       (ij,pqrs,coef) multiset as OF -- only bra_pos/ket_pos labels differ.
  OS   9-CSF subset of s_soci_mid (core2/act2/ext2, nve6, SOCI), input order;
       subspace; full+one. Source rows (0-based) 0,3,15,16,26,29,59,92,101:
         0   efefef   3  efuudd                 (single core hole, orb0)
         15  uuefdd  16  uuuddd  26 udefef  29 uduudd  (double-core-hole sector)
         59  feefef                              (single core hole, orb1)
         92  ffeeef  101 fffeee                  (cores full)

File format (self-describing)
-----------------------------
  norb  <n>
  ncsfs <N>
  csf <i>  <step bytes ...>          # input row i, INPUT order (E/U/D/F=0/1/2/3)
  which full|one
  nterms <count>
  # bra_pos ket_pos ij pqrs coef
  <bra_pos> <ket_pos> <ij> <pqrs> <coef>
  ...

  * bra_pos/ket_pos are INPUT positions (0..N-1): the input-order contract.
  * ij is the canonical triangular bra/ket index; pqrs is the binary-API
    integral key (0-based). Both as the C API returns them.
  * coef printed "%+.16e" (17 significant digits -> lossless double round-trip).
  * Rows sorted by (bra_pos, ket_pos, ij, pqrs), with a stable emit-order
    tiebreak. The consumer applies the identical sort and compares coef
    bit-exact (==) -- same code path today.

Regenerating (transparency only; the committed files ARE the oracle)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
  make -C c/tests gen-csfset-oracle      # rewrites reference_csfset/*.txt
The test does not require regeneration:
  make -C c/tests check                  # runs test_csfset_pair among others
